# Copy to apps/server/.env. Every variable below is parsed and validated by
# src/lib/env.ts at startup — a missing or malformed value stops the process
# before it listens, with the offending field named.

# Runtime mode. One of: development | test | production.
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the API listens on.
PORT=4000

# Runtime database connection (pooled). The Prisma client imported from @kidlearn/db
# reads this at startup. Use the SAME pooled string as packages/db/.env DATABASE_URL
# (Supabase pooler, port 6543, ?pgbouncer=true). Duplicated here intentionally because
# the server runs as a separate process and needs the var in its own environment.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.pidlfhlaxtkpapulsgtj:[YOUR-PASSWORD]@aws-1-ap-southeast-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"

# The single browser origin allowed to call this API with credentials. Exactly
# one origin — no wildcards. Point it at the deployed apps/web URL in production.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# pino log level. One of: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- better-auth / Google OAuth (file 09) ------------------------------------
# Public base URL of THIS server. better-auth derives its OAuth callback from it,
# so it must match the redirect URI registered in Google Cloud exactly.
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Session/OAuth-state signing key. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Minimum 32 characters. Rotating it signs every live parent out.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# Google OAuth client credentials. One-time console setup:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com → create project `kidlearn-dev`.
#   2. APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen → External. App name "KidLearn",
#      add your own email as support contact. Scopes: openid, email, profile.
#      Add yourself under "Test users" while the app is unpublished.
#   3. Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application.
#   4. Authorized JavaScript origins:  http://localhost:3000
#      Authorized redirect URIs:       http://localhost:4000/api/auth/callback/google
#   5. Copy the client ID and secret into the two variables below.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Path on WEB_ORIGIN the browser lands on after a successful sign-in.
PARENT_POST_LOGIN_PATH=/parent

# --- Rewards and streaks (files 23, 27) --------------------------------------
# The IANA timezone every "day" in the product is measured in: the once-a-day
# coin grant and the streak roll-over both read it, so they cannot disagree about
# where midnight is. One zone for the whole deployment — per-child timezones are
# post-MVP. A name the platform's ICU data does not know is rejected at boot.
APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Dhaka

# --- API documentation (file 12a) --------------------------------------------
# Swagger UI at /docs and the raw spec at /docs.json are always available outside
# production. In production they are off unless this is exactly the string "true",
# because the docs describe the entire API surface. Values other than "true" or
# "false" are rejected at boot.
ENABLE_API_DOCS=false

# --- Scheduled jobs (file 30) ------------------------------------------------
# Shared secret guarding POST /api/admin/jobs/weekly-reports. Required — the
# server will not boot without it, deliberately: an optional secret would leave
# the job endpoint open whenever a deployment forgot the variable.
#
# Deliberately NOT an admin session: the caller is an external scheduler with no
# browser. Setup, once the API is deployed:
#   1. openssl rand -base64 32   → paste the result below and into the scheduler.
#   2. cron-job.org → Create cronjob.
#      URL:      https://<render-service>/api/admin/jobs/weekly-reports
#      Method:   POST
#      Header:   Authorization: Bearer <the same value>
#      Schedule: every Monday at 02:00, timezone Asia/Dhaka.
#   3. Enable "Treat redirects as success: no" and leave the timeout generous —
#      Render's free tier cold-starts, and nothing is waiting on this job.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Admin seeding (file 31) --------------------------------------------------
# Read by `pnpm --filter server seed:admin` only — NOT by src/lib/env.ts, and the
# server boots fine without them. Deliberately outside the boot-time schema: a
# deployment must not need an admin password present just to start, and a stale
# password sitting in the server's own environment is a credential nobody rotates.
#
# Admins never self-register — better-auth's password sign-up is disabled for
# every caller (see emailAndPassword in src/lib/auth.ts), so this script is the
# only way an administrator comes into existence. It is also how a forgotten
# password is recovered: re-run it with a new ADMIN_PASSWORD and the credential
# hash is replaced. There is no self-service reset flow.
#
# Prefer passing them inline so they never land in a file:
#   ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com ADMIN_PASSWORD='...' ADMIN_NAME='Your Name' \
#     pnpm --filter server seed:admin
#
# ADMIN_PASSWORD must be at least 12 characters — the floor is enforced by both
# the script and better-auth, because these accounts have no rate limiting until
# file 38 and no second factor at all.
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_NAME=

# --- Cloudinary media host (file 33) ----------------------------------------
# All three are required — the server will not boot without them, deliberately:
# an optional credential would leave the media library answering 500 the first
# time an admin picked a file, with a missing variable as the invisible cause.
#
# Setup, once:
#   1. cloudinary.com → sign up (the free tier is what §9 budgets for).
#   2. Dashboard → Product Environment Credentials. Copy the cloud name, API key
#      and API secret below.
#   3. Settings → Upload → keep "Unsigned uploading" OFF. Every upload this app
#      performs is signed by POST /api/admin/media/sign, so an unsigned preset
#      would be a second, unauthenticated door into the account.
#
# THE SECRET NEVER LEAVES THE SERVER. It signs the upload parameters the admin's
# browser then posts straight to Cloudinary, so no file byte passes through this
# process (FR-CMS-02).
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=

# --- Claude API (file 34, FR-AI-01..03, FR-AI-08) ----------------------------
# The key is required — the server will not boot without it, for the same reason
# the Cloudinary credentials are required: an AI Queue that answers 500 on its
# first use is worse than a process that refuses to start and names the variable.
#
# Setup, once:
#   1. console.anthropic.com → API keys → Create key. Scope it to the workspace
#      this deployment bills to; spend caps and monitoring land with file 38.
#   2. Paste it below. It is read only by apps/server — no key ever reaches the
#      browser, because every generation runs server-side.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Which model the generators call. Optional — defaults to the latest Sonnet-class
# model, which is what the tracker's Shared Technical Decisions fix. Override it
# to pin a specific snapshot or to try a different tier without a code change.
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
